that based on the Provisions in Article 17 paragraph (Il) of Law Number 23 Year 2074 concerning Regional Government as amended by Law Number 1 Year 2022 concerning Financial Relations between the Central Government and Regional Governments, the Region has the right to establish policies for the protection of women and children which constitute mandatory affairs in accordance with its authority;

that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Protection of Women and Children From Acts of Violence.

Article 22

1. Rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 17 paragraph (f) letter e include:a. motivation and psychosocial diagnosis;
b. care and nurturing;
c. vocational training and entrepreneurship development;
d. spiritual mental guidance;
e. physical guidance;
f. social guidance and psychosocial counseling;
g. accessibility services;
h. social assistance and aid;
i. resocialization guidance;
j. follow-up guidance; and/ ork. referral.
2. Forms of vocational training and entrepreneurship development as mentioned in paragraph (1) letter c, include:a. skills training;
b. field work practice;
c. apprenticeship;
d. entrepreneurial skills training;
e. facilitation of joint business group formation;
f. business capital in the form of business support tools; andg. business implementation assistance.

Article 27

1. The community participates in the provision of protection for victims.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (l) is in the form of: a. forming family partners at the RT/RW or Village level by the community;
b. forming Women and Children protection units within community organizations;
c. conducting independent socialization of Women and Children's rights; d. providing first aid to victims; ande. reporting to the relevant authorized Regional Apparatus/institutions if violence against victims occurs in their environment. (3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, mental and spiritual development institutions, the private sector, and mass media.

Article 16

